# Module 06: Interfaces, Default Methods & Polymorphism

> **Source Track:** Oracle Java Tutorials — *Learning the Java Language*  
> **Source Lessons:** `IandI/createinterface.html`, `interfaceDef.html`, `usinginterface.html`, `interfaceAsType.html`, `nogrow.html`, `defaultmethods.html`, `summary-interface.html`  
> **Scope:** Interface Contracts, Implementing Interfaces, Polymorphic References, Interface Evolution, `default` Methods, `static` & `private` Interface Methods, Multiple Interface Inheritance Conflict Resolution Rules.

---

## 1. Architectural Overview & Contractual Abstraction

An **Interface** in Java is a reference type that serves as a formal contract between a service provider and client callers. 
- Prior to Java 8, interfaces were strictly 100% abstract: they could only contain constant definitions (`public static final`) and method signatures (`public abstract`).
- Modern Java allows interfaces to carry behavioral logic via **`default` methods**, utility routines via **`static` methods**, and encapsulation helpers via **`private` methods**.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    subgraph InterfaceElements ["Anatomy of a Modern Java Interface"]
        C["Constants: public static final"]
        AM["Abstract Methods: public abstract"]
        DM["Default Methods: default void execute() { ... }"]
        SM["Static Methods: static void util() { ... }"]
        PM["Private Methods: private void helper() { ... } (Java 9+)"]
    end
```

### Why Program to an Interface?
1. **Loose Coupling:** The client depends on the abstract contract rather than concrete implementation details.
2. **Polymorphic Substitutability:** Concrete implementations can be swapped (e.g. `ArrayList` vs `LinkedList`) without altering client business logic.
3. **Multiple Behavioral Extension:** Unlike single class inheritance, a Java class can implement multiple independent interfaces (`implements Serializable, Cloneable, Comparable<T>`).

---

## 2. Evolving Interfaces: Default, Static & Private Methods

### The Evolution Dilemma
Adding a new abstract method to an existing public interface in a library immediately breaks all third-party classes implementing that interface upon compilation.

### The Solution: `default` Methods
A method declared with the `default` keyword provides an inline fallback implementation body. Existing implementing classes automatically inherit this default behavior without requiring code changes.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    I["interface Collection&lt;E&gt;<br/>+ default Stream&lt;E&gt; stream() { ... }"]
    Legacy["class LegacyCustomList implements Collection&lt;E&gt;"]
    Modern["class ModernFastList implements Collection&lt;E&gt;"]

    I -->|"Inherits fallback stream() logic"| Legacy
    I -->|"Overrides stream() with custom spliterator"| Modern
```

### Static & Private Methods in Interfaces
- **`static` Methods:** Utility functions scoped directly to the interface namespace (e.g., `Comparator.comparingInt(...)`). They are not inherited by implementing classes.
- **`private` Methods (Java 9+):** Allows multiple default methods within the same interface to share common implementation code without exposing helper logic to the public API.

---

## 3. Multiple Interface Inheritance & Conflict Resolution

When a class implements multiple interfaces that declare default methods with identical signatures, Java enforces **Three Concrete Conflict Resolution Rules**:

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Conflict{"Conflict: Both Interface A and Interface B define default void act()"}
    Conflict --> R1{"Rule 1: Does a Superclass provide an implementation?"}
    R1 -->|Yes| SuperClassWins["Superclass Method Wins! (Class always beats Interface)"]
    R1 -->|No| R2{"Rule 2: Is one Interface a sub-interface of the other?"}
    R2 -->|Yes| SubInterfaceWins["Most Specific Interface Wins!"]
    R2 -->|No| R3["Rule 3: Compiler Error! Must explicitly disambiguate via:<br/>InterfaceA.super.act();"]
```

### The Three Invariants
1. **Rule 1 (Classes Win):** A method declaration in a superclass takes precedence over any `default` method declared in any implemented interface.
2. **Rule 2 (Sub-interfaces Win):** If neither method is in a superclass, the method in the most specific interface wins (e.g., if `B extends A`, `B`'s default method overrides `A`'s).
3. **Rule 3 (Explicit Disambiguation):** If the conflict remains ambiguous between two unrelated interfaces, the compiler fails with a collision error. The implementor must explicitly override the method and specify which super-interface logic to invoke:
   ```java
   @Override
   public void act() {
       InterfaceA.super.act(); // Explicit qualification
   }
   ```

---

## 4. Exhaustive Member Function & Interface Reference

| Category | Syntax / Construct (Example) | Use Case (Code Snippet) | Specification | Description & Behavioral Invariant |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Interface Definition**| `public interface Sorter<T>` | `public interface Cache<K, V> { ... }` | Type declaration | Declares contractual API; all fields are implicitly `public static final`. |
| **Abstract Method** | `void process(Data d);` | `boolean evict(K key);` | Implicitly abstract | Contractual method signature that must be implemented by concrete classes. |
| **Default Method** | `default void log(String s)` | `default boolean isEmpty() { return size() == 0; }` | Inherited fallback | Provides default behavior across all implementors without breaking backwards compatibility. |
| **Static Interface Method**| `static Sorter natural()` | `static <T> Comparator<T> reverseOrder()` | Namespace utility | Bound directly to interface type; called as `InterfaceName.method()`. |
| **Private Method** | `private void logInternal()` | `private void validateKey(K k) { ... }` | Java 9+ helper | Encapsulated helper subroutine shared among default methods within the interface. |
| **Super Interface Call**| `Interface.super.method()` | `Flyable.super.takeoff();` | O(1) explicit call | Resolves diamond inheritance conflicts by delegating to a specific parent interface. |
| **Polymorphic Reference**| `List<String> l = new ArrayList<>()`| `Queue<Task> q = new ArrayDeque<>();` | Loose coupling | References object through interface type, decoupling client from storage implementation. |

---

## 5. Modern Idiomatic Java Code Implementation

```java
// Comprehensive demonstration of modern interfaces, default methods, and conflict resolution
package devtype.engine.interfaces;

public final class InterfaceLab {

    public interface Encryptor {
        byte[] encrypt(byte[] data);

        // Default method providing convenient String overloads
        default String encryptString(String input) {
            if (input == null) return null;
            byte[] cipher = encrypt(input.getBytes());
            return encodeBase64Internal(cipher);
        }

        // Static factory method
        static Encryptor noOp() {
            return data -> data; // Lambda implementing SAM encrypt()
        }

        // Private helper method shared among default methods (Java 9+)
        private String encodeBase64Internal(byte[] bytes) {
            return java.util.Base64.getEncoder().encodeToString(bytes);
        }
    }

    public interface Auditable {
        default void logAudit(String event) {
            System.out.println("[AUDIT]: " + event);
        }
    }

    public interface SuperAuditable extends Auditable {
        @Override
        default void logAudit(String event) {
            System.out.println("[SUPER-AUDIT (High Priority)]: " + event);
        }
    }

    // Demonstrates Rule 2: Most specific interface (SuperAuditable) wins automatically!
    public static class SecurityService implements Auditable, SuperAuditable {
        public void performAction() {
            logAudit("Action executed"); // Calls SuperAuditable's implementation
        }
    }

    // Demonstrates Rule 3: Explicit disambiguation when peer interfaces collide
    public interface LeftChannel {
        default void transmit() { System.out.println("Left"); }
    }

    public interface RightChannel {
        default void transmit() { System.out.println("Right"); }
    }

    public static class DualTransceiver implements LeftChannel, RightChannel {
        @Override
        public void transmit() {
            // Must explicitly resolve collision
            LeftChannel.super.transmit();
            RightChannel.super.transmit();
        }
    }
}
```

---

## 6. Common Traps, Anti-Patterns & Best Practices

1. **Attempting to Store Instance State in Interfaces:**
   - *Trap:* Declaring a field `int count = 0;` inside an interface does NOT create instance state. All interface fields are implicitly `public static final` constants.
   - *Remedy:* If mutable instance state is required, use an `abstract class`.

2. **Accidentally Narrowing Access Modifiers on Implementation:**
   - *Trap:* Writing `void method()` in the implementing class causes a compile error: *"attempting to assign weaker access privileges; was public"*.
   - *Remedy:* Interface methods are implicitly `public`, so implementing methods must explicitly specify `public`.

3. **Treating Default Methods as Substitutes for True Domain Inheritance:**
   - *Trap:* Bloating interfaces with state-dependent logic via default methods leads to fragile designs and unexpected diamond conflicts.
   - *Remedy:* Keep default methods focused on optional convenience overloads or non-breaking API evolution.

code:
package devtype.engine.interfaces;

public final class InterfaceLab {

    public interface Encryptor {
        byte[] encrypt(byte[] data);

        default String encryptString(String input) {
            if (input == null) return null;
            byte[] cipher = encrypt(input.getBytes());
            return java.util.Base64.getEncoder().encodeToString(cipher);
        }

        static Encryptor noOp() {
            return data -> data;
        }
    }

    public interface LeftChannel {
        default void transmit() { System.out.println("Left"); }
    }

    public interface RightChannel {
        default void transmit() { System.out.println("Right"); }
    }

    public static class DualTransceiver implements LeftChannel, RightChannel {
        @Override
        public void transmit() {
            LeftChannel.super.transmit();
            RightChannel.super.transmit();
        }
    }
}

