# Module 07: Inheritance, Overriding & The Root Object Class

> **Source Track:** Oracle Java Tutorials — *Learning the Java Language*  
> **Source Lessons:** `IandI/subclasses.html`, `multipleinheritance.html`, `override.html`, `polymorphism.html`, `hidevariables.html`, `super.html`, `objectclass.html`, `final.html`, `abstract.html`, `summaryinherit.html`  
> **Scope:** Subclassing, Method Overriding Invariants, Covariant Return Types, Polymorphic Dynamic Dispatch (vtable), Field & Static Method Hiding, `super`, The `Object` Contract (`equals`, `hashCode`, `toString`), `final` vs `abstract`.

---

## 1. Architectural Overview: Single Implementation Inheritance

Java enforces **Strict Single Class Inheritance**: a class can directly extend exactly one superclass (`class Sub extends Super`). Every class in the Java ecosystem (except `java.lang.Object` itself) implicitly or explicitly descends from the root `Object` class.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Obj["java.lang.Object<br/>- equals()<br/>- hashCode()<br/>- toString()<br/>- getClass()"]
    Shape["abstract class Shape<br/>+ abstract double area()"]
    Circle["class Circle extends Shape<br/>+ double area() [Overridden]"]
    Square["class Square extends Shape<br/>+ double area() [Overridden]"]

    Obj --> Shape
    Shape --> Circle
    Shape --> Square
```

---

## 2. Dynamic Method Dispatch & The Virtual Method Table (vtable)

When a method is called on an object reference (`shape.area()`), Java uses **Dynamic Method Dispatch** (late binding) to execute the implementation corresponding to the actual **runtime object instance**, rather than the compile-time reference type.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    Ref["Shape ref = new Circle(); (Compile-time Type: Shape)"]
    Ref -->|Points to Heap Instance| Instance["Circle Instance (Runtime Type: Circle)"]
    Instance -->|Klass Pointer| CircleMeta["Circle Metaspace Metadata"]
    CircleMeta --> VTable["Circle Virtual Method Table (vtable)<br/>[0] equals -> Object.equals<br/>[1] toString -> Object.toString<br/>[2] area() -> Circle.area()"]
    VTable -->|Executes| Exec["Circle.area() Bytecode"]
```

### Method Overriding Rules & Invariants
1. **Signature Equivalence:** Must match the exact method name and parameter types.
2. **Covariant Return Type:** The return type may be the same or a **subtype** of the return type declared in the superclass.
3. **Access Privilege Preservation:** The access modifier cannot be made more restrictive (e.g., a `protected` method in superclass cannot be overridden as `private` or package-private).
4. **Exception Specification:** The overriding method cannot throw new or broader **checked exceptions** than those declared by the superclass method.

---

## 3. Hiding Fields vs. Overriding Methods

> [!WARNING]
> **Fields in Java are NOT polymorphic.**  
> If a subclass declares a field with the same name as a superclass field, the subclass field **hides** the superclass field. Access is determined purely by the **compile-time declared type of the reference variable**, NOT the runtime object.

```java
class Super { int x = 10; }
class Sub extends Super { int x = 20; }

Super ref = new Sub();
System.out.println(ref.x); // Prints 10 (resolved by static reference type)!
```

Similarly, **`static` methods cannot be overridden**; they are hidden. Static method invocations are resolved at compile time based on the declared reference type.

---

## 4. The Fundamental `java.lang.Object` Contract

Every class inherits the foundational contract methods of `java.lang.Object`:

### The `equals()` Contract
Must be:
1. **Reflexive:** `x.equals(x)` must return `true`.
2. **Symmetric:** `x.equals(y)` returns `true` if and only if `y.equals(x)` returns `true`.
3. **Transitive:** If `x.equals(y)` and `y.equals(z)`, then `x.equals(z)` must return `true`.
4. **Consistent:** Repeated invocations return the same result unless state is mutated.
5. **Null-Safe:** For any non-null reference `x`, `x.equals(null)` must return `false`.

### The `hashCode()` Invariant
- **Rule 1 (Consistency):** The hash code must remain identical during execution if fields used in `equals()` are unmutated.
- **Rule 2 (Equality Guarantee):** If `x.equals(y) == true`, then `x.hashCode() == y.hashCode()` **MUST ALWAYS BE TRUE**.
- **Rule 3 (Inequality Collision):** If `x.equals(y) == false`, their hash codes do *not* have to be distinct (though distinct hash codes improve hash table performance).

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A["Obj A"] ---|"equals() == true"| B["Obj B"]
    A -.->|"hashCode()"| H1["Hash: 48291"]
    B -.->|"hashCode()"| H2["Hash: 48291 (MUST BE IDENTICAL!)"]
```

---

## 5. Exhaustive Member Function & Contract Reference

| Category | Method / Syntax (Example) | Use Case (Code Snippet) | Complexity | Description & Behavioral Invariant |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Super Method Call** | `super.method(args)` | `super.paintComponent(g);` | O(1) direct call | Invokes the overridden superclass method from within the subclass. |
| **Equals Contract** | `boolean equals(Object o)`| `if (this == o) return true;` | O(1) to O(N) | Tests logical equivalence of internal states; must satisfy equivalence relations. |
| **HashCode Contract** | `int hashCode()` | `return Objects.hash(id, name);` | O(1) calculation | Generates integer hash for hash table bucket distribution; bound to `equals`. |
| **String Representation**| `String toString()` | `return "Point[" + x + ", " + y + "]";` | O(N) string build| Formats a human-readable diagnostic representation of the object. |
| **Class Token** | `Class<?> getClass()` | `if (o.getClass() != getClass()) return false;` | O(1) header lookup | Returns the runtime `Class` metadata token directly from the object's Klass Word. |
| **Final Class** | `public final class Token`| `public final class ImmutablePair { ... }` | Compile check | Prevents any subclassing; enables JVM compiler inlining optimizations. |
| **Abstract Method** | `abstract double area();` | `public abstract void render();` | Virtual dispatch | Defines a polymorphic signature that concrete subclasses must implement. |

---

## 6. Modern Idiomatic Java Code Implementation

```java
// Comprehensive demonstration of inheritance, covariant returns, and equals/hashCode
package devtype.engine.inheritance;

import java.util.Objects;

public final class InheritanceLab {

    public static abstract class Shape {
        private final String color;

        public Shape(String color) {
            this.color = Objects.requireNonNull(color, "Color cannot be null");
        }

        public String getColor() { return color; }

        public abstract double area();

        // Overridable copy method demonstrating covariant return types in subclasses
        public abstract Shape copy();
    }

    public static class Circle extends Shape {
        private final double radius;

        public Circle(String color, double radius) {
            super(color);
            if (radius <= 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Radius must be positive");
            this.radius = radius;
        }

        public double getRadius() { return radius; }

        @Override
        public double area() {
            return Math.PI * radius * radius;
        }

        // Covariant return: returns Circle instead of generic Shape
        @Override
        public Circle copy() {
            return new Circle(this.getColor(), this.radius);
        }

        // Robust, canonical implementation of equals and hashCode
        @Override
        public boolean equals(Object o) {
            if (this == o) return true; // Reference identity optimization
            if (o == null || getClass() != o.getClass()) return false; // Exact type check
            Circle circle = (Circle) o;
            return Double.compare(circle.radius, radius) == 0 &&
                   Objects.equals(getColor(), circle.getColor());
        }

        @Override
        public int hashCode() {
            return Objects.hash(getColor(), radius);
        }

        @Override
        public String toString() {
            return String.format("Circle[color=%s, radius=%.2f, area=%.2f]", getColor(), radius, area());
        }
    }
}
```

---

## 7. Common Traps, Anti-Patterns & Best Practices

1. **Overriding `equals()` without Overriding `hashCode()`:**
   - *Trap:* Adding equal objects to a `HashSet` or as keys in a `HashMap` results in duplicate entries or inability to retrieve items because they map to different hash buckets.
   - *Remedy:* Always override both methods together using `Objects.hash(...)`.

2. **Calling Overridable Methods Inside Constructors:**
   - *Trap:* Invoking an overridden method inside a superclass constructor causes the subclass method to execute *before* the subclass fields have been initialized, operating on uninitialized `null`/`0` values!
   - *Remedy:* Never call non-final, overridable methods inside constructors.

3. **Confusing Overriding with Overloading in Subclasses:**
   - *Trap:* Writing `public boolean equals(Circle o)` instead of `public boolean equals(Object o)` **overloads** rather than overrides the method, causing collections to bypass it.
   - *Remedy:* Always use the `@Override` annotation; the compiler will flag accidental overloading immediately.

code:
package devtype.engine.inheritance;

import java.util.Objects;

public final class InheritanceLab {

    public static abstract class Shape {
        private final String color;

        public Shape(String color) {
            this.color = Objects.requireNonNull(color, "Color cannot be null");
        }

        public String getColor() { return color; }
        public abstract double area();
        public abstract Shape copy();
    }

    public static class Circle extends Shape {
        private final double radius;

        public Circle(String color, double radius) {
            super(color);
            if (radius <= 0) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Radius must be positive");
            this.radius = radius;
        }

        @Override
        public double area() { return Math.PI * radius * radius; }

        @Override
        public Circle copy() { return new Circle(getColor(), this.radius); }

        @Override
        public boolean equals(Object o) {
            if (this == o) return true;
            if (o == null || getClass() != o.getClass()) return false;
            Circle circle = (Circle) o;
            return Double.compare(circle.radius, radius) == 0 &&
                   Objects.equals(getColor(), circle.getColor());
        }

        @Override
        public int hashCode() {
            return Objects.hash(getColor(), radius);
        }
    }
}

package devtype.engine.text;

import java.util.List;

public final class TextNumbersLab {

    public static long calculateSafeCapacity(int users, int quotaPerUser) {
        try {
            return Math.multiplyExact((long) users, (long) quotaPerUser);
        } catch (ArithmeticException e) {
            return Long.MAX_VALUE;
        }
    }

    public static String buildCsvReport(List<String> headers, List<List<String>> rows) {
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(1024);
        sb.append(String.join(",", headers)).append("
");

        for (List<String> row : rows) {
            for (int i = 0; i < row.size(); i++) {
                String cell = row.get(i);
                if (cell != null && cell.contains(",")) {
                    sb.append('"').append(cell).append('"');
                } else {
                    sb.append(cell != null ? cell.strip() : "");
                }
                if (i < row.size() - 1) sb.append(',');
            }
            sb.append("
");
        }
        return sb.toString();
    }
}

