# Module 09: Generics, Wildcards (PECS) & Type Erasure

> **Source Track:** Oracle Java Tutorials — *Learning the Java Language*  
> **Source Lessons:** `generics/why.html`, `types.html`, `rawTypes.html`, `methods.html`, `bounded.html`, `inheritance.html`, `genTypeInference.html`, `wildcards.html`, `upperBounded.html`, `unboundedWildcards.html`, `lowerBounded.html`, `subtyping.html`, `capture.html`, `wildcardGuidelines.html`, `erasure.html`, `bridgeMethods.html`, `restrictions.html`  
> **Scope:** Generic Classes/Methods, Invariance vs Covariance, Wildcards, The PECS Principle (Producer Extends, Consumer Super), Wildcard Capture, Type Erasure, Bridge Methods, Generic Restrictions.

---

## 1. Architectural Overview: Compile-Time Safety via Erasure

Java Generics were introduced in Java 5 to provide **strong compile-time type checking** and eliminate explicit type casting.
- **Key Design Constraint:** To ensure 100% binary backwards-compatibility with legacy pre-Java 5 bytecode, generics are implemented via **Type Erasure**.
- Type parameters exist purely at compile time; the JVM runtime bytecode has **no knowledge of generic type arguments**.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    subgraph CompileTime ["Compile-Time (Java Compiler)"]
        Source["List&lt;String&gt; list = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();<br/>list.add('text');<br/>String s = list.get(0);"]
    end

    subgraph BytecodeRun ["Runtime Bytecode (HotSpot JVM)"]
        Erased["List list = new ArrayList();<br/>list.add('text');<br/>String s = (String) list.get(0); // Synthetic cast inserted!"]
    end

    Source -->|"Type Erasure & Cast Insertion"| Erased
```

---

## 2. Invariance of Generics vs. Array Covariance

In Java, **arrays are covariant**, but **generics are invariant**:
- **Array Covariance (Unsafe):** `Integer[]` is considered a subtype of `Number[]`. You can pass `Integer[]` where `Number[]` is expected, which permits attempting to store a `Double` into an `Integer[]`, crashing at runtime with `java.lang.ArrayStoreException`!
- **Generic Invariance (Safe):** `List<Integer>` is **NOT** a subtype of `List<Number>`. The compiler rejects assigning `List<Integer>` to `List<Number>`, preventing runtime type corruption entirely.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    subgraph ArrayCovariance ["Array Covariance (Runtime Hazard)"]
        ArrNum["Number[] arr = new Integer[5];"]
        ArrAssign["arr[0] = 3.14; // Throws ArrayStoreException at runtime!"]
        ArrNum --> ArrAssign
    end

    subgraph GenericInvariance ["Generic Invariance (Compile-Time Safe)"]
        GenErr["List&lt;Number&gt; list = new ArrayList&lt;Integer&gt;();<br/>// COMPILE-TIME ERROR: Incompatible types!"]
    end
```

---

## 3. Wildcards & The PECS Principle

To restore flexibility while maintaining type safety, Java provides **Wildcard Arguments (`?`)**:

### The PECS Guideline: **Producer Extends, Consumer Super**
- **Producer Extends (`? extends T`):** Use an upper bounded wildcard when your method reads data *from* a generic structure (the structure produces values of type `T`). You can read `T` safely, but **cannot write** anything (except `null`).
- **Consumer Super (`? super T`):** Use a lower bounded wildcard when your method writes data *into* a generic structure (the structure consumes values of type `T`). You can safely write instances of `T`, but reading only yields `Object`.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    PECS["The PECS Guideline"]
    PECS --> Prod["PRODUCER (Read-Only) -> ? extends T<br/>List&lt;? extends Number&gt;<br/>Number n = list.get(i); // OK<br/>list.add(10); // COMPILE ERROR!"]
    PECS --> Cons["CONSUMER (Write-Only) -> ? super T<br/>List&lt;? super Integer&gt;<br/>list.add(10); // OK<br/>Integer i = list.get(0); // COMPILE ERROR (yields Object)!"]
```

---

## 4. Type Erasure & Synthetic Bridge Methods

### What Happens During Erasure?
1. The compiler replaces each type parameter `<T>` with its first bound, or `Object` if unbounded.
2. Injects synthetic checkcast instructions wherever an element is extracted from a generic type.
3. Synthesizes **Bridge Methods** in derived classes to preserve polymorphism and covariant method overriding at the bytecode level.

```mermaid
classDiagram
    class Node {
        +setData(Object obj)
    }
    class MyNode {
        +setData(Integer obj)
        +setData(Object obj) synthetic bridge method
    }
    Node <|-- MyNode
```

---

## 5. The Seven Hard Restrictions on Generics

Because of Type Erasure, the following operations are strictly illegal in Java:
1. **Cannot instantiate type parameters with primitive types:** `List<int>` is invalid; must use wrapper `List<Integer>`.
2. **Cannot create instances of type parameters:** `new T()` is invalid (type is erased to `Object` at runtime).
3. **Cannot declare static fields whose types are type parameters:** `private static T shared;` is invalid because static fields are shared across all instances regardless of parameterization.
4. **Cannot use casts or `instanceof` with parameterized types:** `if (obj instanceof List<String>)` is invalid because parameterization is erased; must use `instanceof List<?>`.
5. **Cannot create arrays of parameterized types:** `new List<String>[10]` is invalid.
6. **Cannot create, catch, or throw objects of parameterized `Throwable` types:** `class GenericException<T> extends Exception` is invalid.
7. **Cannot overload methods where formal parameter types erase to the same raw type:** `void print(List<String> l)` and `void print(List<Integer> l)` cannot coexist in the same class.

---

## 6. Exhaustive Member Function & Generics Reference

| Category | Syntax / Construct (Example) | Use Case (Code Snippet) | Specification | Description & Behavioral Invariant |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Generic Class** | `class Box<T>` | `public class PriorityEntry<K, V> { ... }`| Type parameterized| Defines a class operating on an abstract type parameter. |
| **Bounded Parameter** | `<T extends Comparable<T>>`| `public static <T extends Number> double sum(T a, T b)` | Upper bound | Restricts type argument to subtypes of the specified class/interfaces. |
| **Multiple Bounds** | `<T extends A & B>` | `<T extends Number & Comparable<T>>` | Intersection bound| Constrains parameter to satisfy both bounds; class must appear first. |
| **Upper Bounded Wildcard**| `? extends Number` | `public static double sum(List<? extends Number> list)` | Producer (Read) | Permits passing `List<Integer>`, `List<Double>`; read-only access. |
| **Lower Bounded Wildcard**| `? super Integer` | `public static void addNums(List<? super Integer> list)` | Consumer (Write) | Permits passing `List<Integer>`, `List<Number>`, `List<Object>`; write-safe. |
| **Unbounded Wildcard** | `List<?>` | `public static void printList(List<?> list)` | Unknown type | Matches any list; elements can only be read as `Object`. |
| **Safe Varargs** | `@SafeVarargs` | `@SafeVarargs public static <T> List<T> asList(T... a)`| Heap pollution suppression | Asserts that generic varargs parameter will not pollute caller heap. |

---

## 7. Modern Idiomatic Java Code Implementation

```java
// Comprehensive demonstration of generic algorithms and the PECS principle in Java 21
package devtype.engine.generics;

import java.util.*;

public final class GenericsLab {

    // Demonstrates PECS: src produces (extends), dest consumes (super)
    public static <T> void copy(List<? extends T> src, List<? super T> dest) {
        for (T item : src) {
            dest.add(item); // Safe write to consumer
        }
    }

    // Generic max algorithm with recursive type bounds
    public static <T extends Comparable<? super T>> T findMax(Collection<? extends T> items) {
        if (items.isEmpty()) {
            throw new NoSuchElementException("Cannot find max of empty collection");
        }

        Iterator<? extends T> it = items.iterator();
        T maxCandidate = it.next();

        while (it.hasNext()) {
            T current = it.next();
            if (current.compareTo(maxCandidate) > 0) {
                maxCandidate = current;
            }
        }

        return maxCandidate;
    }

    // Wildcard capture pattern using a private generic helper
    public static void reverseList(List<?> list) {
        reverseHelper(list);
    }

    private static <T> void reverseHelper(List<T> list) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = list.size() - 1;
        while (left < right) {
            T temp = list.get(left);
            list.set(left, list.get(right));
            list.set(right, temp);
            left++;
            right--;
        }
    }
}
```

---

## 8. Common Traps, Anti-Patterns & Best Practices

1. **Attempting to Write to a Producer (`? extends T`):**
   - *Trap:* Calling `list.add(item)` on a `List<? extends Number>` fails compilation because the compiler cannot guarantee the concrete type of the list at runtime.
   - *Remedy:* If writing elements, change the parameter wildcard to `? super T`.

2. **Heap Pollution with Generic Varargs:**
   - *Trap:* Combining generics with varargs (`void process(List<T>... lists)`) implicitly creates a generic array (`List<T>[]`), causing unchecked heap pollution compiler warnings.
   - *Remedy:* Annotate the method with `@SafeVarargs` only after verifying that the array is never written to or escaped.

3. **Using Raw Types in Modern Code:**
   - *Trap:* Declaring `List list = new ArrayList();` bypasses compile-time type checking, reintroducing runtime `ClassCastException` hazards.
   - *Remedy:* Always use parameterized types (`List<String>`) or diamond operators (`new ArrayList<>()`).

code:
package devtype.engine.generics;

import java.util.*;

public final class GenericsLab {

    public static <T> void copy(List<? extends T> src, List<? super T> dest) {
        for (T item : src) {
            dest.add(item); // Safe consumer write
        }
    }

    public static <T extends Comparable<? super T>> T findMax(Collection<? extends T> items) {
        if (items.isEmpty()) throw new NoSuchElementException("Empty collection");
        Iterator<? extends T> it = items.iterator();
        T maxCandidate = it.next();

        while (it.hasNext()) {
            T current = it.next();
            if (current.compareTo(maxCandidate) > 0) {
                maxCandidate = current;
            }
        }
        return maxCandidate;
    }

    public static void reverseList(List<?> list) {
        reverseHelper(list);
    }

    private static <T> void reverseHelper(List<T> list) {
        int left = 0, right = list.size() - 1;
        while (left < right) {
            T temp = list.get(left);
            list.set(left, list.get(right));
            list.set(right, temp);
            left++; right--;
        }
    }
}

package devtype.engine.meta;

import java.lang.annotation.*;
import java.lang.reflect.Method;

public final class AnnotationLab {

    @Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
    @Target(ElementType.METHOD)
    @Documented
    public @interface TimedOperation {
        String name();
        int warningThresholdMs() default 100;
        boolean critical() default false;
    }

    public static class DatabaseService {
        @TimedOperation(name = "UserFetchQuery", warningThresholdMs = 50, critical = true)
        public void executeQuery() {
            try { Thread.sleep(20); } catch (InterruptedException ignored) {}
        }
    }

    public static void runDiagnosticScan(Class<?> targetClass) {
        for (Method method : targetClass.getDeclaredMethods()) {
            if (method.isAnnotationPresent(TimedOperation.class)) {
                TimedOperation ann = method.getAnnotation(TimedOperation.class);
                System.out.printf("Monitored: '%s' | Threshold: %dms | Critical: %b%n",
                        ann.name(), ann.warningThresholdMs(), ann.critical());
            }
        }
    }
}

