# Module 13: Exceptions III — Exception Chaining, Custom Exceptions & Translation

> **Source Track:** Oracle Java Tutorials — *Essential Java Classes*  
> **Source Lessons:** `exceptions/throwing.html`, `chained.html`, `creating.html`, `summary.html`  
> **Scope:** Throwing Exceptions, Chained Exceptions (`initCause()`, `getCause()`), Exception Translation, Designing Custom Checked vs Unchecked Exceptions.

---

## 1. Architectural Overview & Exception Translation

In layered software architectures (e.g., Data Access $\rightarrow$ Domain Service $\rightarrow$ REST API), low-level technical exceptions (e.g., `java.sql.SQLException`) should not leak into high-level business layers.
- **Exception Translation:** Catching a low-level implementation exception and throwing a higher-level domain-specific exception.
- **Exception Chaining:** Attaching the original low-level exception as the **cause** of the new domain exception, preserving the root diagnostic stack trace.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Low["Low-Level Fault: SQLException (Connection Refused)"] --> Catch["Data Layer catches SQLException"]
    Catch --> Wrap["Translate to Domain Exception:<br/>throw new RepositoryException('Failed to query user', sqlEx);"]
    Wrap --> High["Service Layer receives RepositoryException<br/>Root cause preserved in getCause()!"]
```

---

## 2. Designing Custom Exception Classes

### Checked vs. Unchecked Rule of Thumb
- **Extend `java.lang.Exception` (Checked):** If the client can reasonably be expected to recover from the exceptional condition (e.g., `InsufficientFundsException`).
- **Extend `java.lang.RuntimeException` (Unchecked):** If the failure represents a programming bug, contract violation, or unrecoverable system condition (e.g., `EntityNotFoundException`).

### Standard Constructor Signatures
Every production custom exception should provide four standard constructor overloads:
1. `CustomException()`: Parameterless default.
2. `CustomException(String message)`: Diagnostic message.
3. `CustomException(String message, Throwable cause)`: Message and chained cause.
4. `CustomException(Throwable cause)`: Chained cause.

---

## 3. Exhaustive Member Function & Chaining Reference

| Category | Method / Syntax (Example) | Use Case (Code Snippet) | Complexity | Description & Behavioral Invariant |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Throw Exception** | `throw new AppEx("msg")` | `if (id == null) throw new IllegalArgumentException();` | $O(1)$ throw | Abruptly transfers control up call stack to nearest matching catch handler. |
| **Exception Chaining**| `new Ex("msg", cause)` | `throw new ServiceException("Storage error", ioEx);` | $O(1)$ object build | Wraps underlying root cause into high-level domain exception. |
| **Get Cause** | `ex.getCause()` | `Throwable root = ex.getCause();` | $O(1)$ pointer read | Extracts the chained underlying exception; returns `null` if none. |
| **Init Cause** | `ex.initCause(cause)` | `ex.initCause(legacyError);` | $O(1)$ mutation | Sets cause on an exception instance that lacks a cause constructor (can only be called once). |

---

## 4. Modern Idiomatic Java Code Implementation

```java
// Comprehensive demonstration of custom domain exceptions and root-cause chaining
package devtype.engine.exceptions;

public final class CustomExceptionLab {

    public static class PaymentGatewayException extends Exception {
        private final String transactionId;

        public PaymentGatewayException(String message, String txId, Throwable cause) {
            super(message + " [TxID: " + txId + "]", cause);
            this.transactionId = txId;
        }

        public String getTransactionId() { return transactionId; }
    }

    public static void executePayment(String txId) throws PaymentGatewayException {
        try {
            // Emulates low-level connection timeout
            throw new java.net.ConnectException("Gateway timed out at 10.0.0.1:443");
        } catch (java.net.ConnectException e) {
            // Exception translation with root-cause preservation
            throw new PaymentGatewayException("Payment authorization failed", txId, e);
        }
    }
}
```

---

## 5. Common Traps, Anti-Patterns & Best Practices

1. **Losing Root Cause During Translation:**
   - *Trap:* Writing `catch (SQLException e) { throw new ServiceException("DB failed"); }` completely erases the root cause stack trace.
   - *Remedy:* Always pass the caught exception as the `cause` parameter: `new ServiceException("DB failed", e)`.

2. **Over-Checked Exceptions Anti-Pattern:**
   - *Trap:* Declaring checked exceptions for every minor validation failure clutters client code with boilerplate try-catch blocks.
   - *Remedy:* Prefer standard unchecked runtime exceptions (`IllegalArgumentException`, `IllegalStateException`) for contract preconditions.

code:
package devtype.engine.exceptions;

public final class CustomExceptionLab {

    public static class PaymentGatewayException extends Exception {
        private final String transactionId;

        public PaymentGatewayException(String message, String txId, Throwable cause) {
            super(message + " [TxID: " + txId + "]", cause);
            this.transactionId = txId;
        }

        public String getTransactionId() { return transactionId; }
    }

    public static void executePayment(String txId) throws PaymentGatewayException {
        try {
            throw new java.net.ConnectException("Gateway timed out at 10.0.0.1:443");
        } catch (java.net.ConnectException e) {
            // Exception translation with root-cause preservation
            throw new PaymentGatewayException("Payment authorization failed", txId, e);
        }
    }
}

