# Module 14: Basic I/O — Byte & Character Streams, Buffers & Object Serialization

> **Source Track:** Oracle Java Tutorials — *Essential Java Classes*  
> **Source Lessons:** `io/streams.html`, `bytestreams.html`, `charstreams.html`, `buffers.html`, `scanfor.html`, `datastreams.html`, `objectstreams.html`  
> **Scope:** Byte Streams vs Character Streams, UTF-8 Transcoding, Buffered Decorator Pattern, Data Streams, Object Serialization Graph, `Serializable`, `transient`, `serialVersionUID`.

---

## 1. Architectural Overview & Stream Taxonomy

Java I/O operations are modeled around **Streams**: sequential data pipelines. The architecture is partitioned by data granularity:
1. **Byte Streams (`InputStream` / `OutputStream`):** 8-bit raw binary data.
2. **Character Streams (`Reader` / `Writer`):** 16-bit Unicode characters with automatic charset transcoding.
3. **Buffered Decorators:** Wrap streams with an internal memory buffer (default 8,192 bytes), dramatically reducing OS syscall overhead.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
    Disk["Raw File Bytes on Disk"] --> FIS["FileInputStream (1 byte per read)"]
    FIS --> BIS["BufferedInputStream (8KB internal chunk buffer)"]
    BIS --> DIS["DataInputStream (Binary primitives: readInt(), readDouble())"]
```

---

## 2. Object Serialization Mechanics

**Serialization** transforms live Heap object graphs into a portable binary byte sequence via `ObjectOutputStream`.
- Requires implementing `java.io.Serializable` (marker interface).
- **The `transient` Keyword:** Excludes sensitive or non-serializable fields (passwords, thread handles) from serialization.
- **`serialVersionUID`:** 64-bit class version identifier ensuring sender and receiver have compatible class definitions.

---

## 3. Exhaustive Member Function & Stream Reference

| Category | Method / Syntax (Example) | Use Case (Code Snippet) | Complexity | Description & Behavioral Invariant |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Byte Read** | `in.read()` | `int b = in.read(); // 0-255 or -1 for EOF` | $O(1)$ stream read | Reads next raw byte of data; returns -1 upon end of stream. |
| **Buffered Read** | `reader.readLine()` | `String line = bufferedReader.readLine();` | $O(Line)$ buffered | Reads a line of text, stripping newline terminators. |
| **Data Primitive** | `out.writeInt(val)` | `dataOut.writeInt(42); dataOut.writeDouble(3.14);`| $O(1)$ binary write | Writes portable binary representation of primitives directly. |
| **Object Write** | `out.writeObject(obj)`| `objectOut.writeObject(sessionGraph);` | $O(Graph)$ graph walk | Serializes entire reachable object graph to output stream. |
| **Object Read** | `(Type) in.readObject()`| `User u = (User) objectIn.readObject();` | $O(Graph)$ deserialization | Reconstructs Heap object graph; throws `ClassNotFoundException`. |

---

## 4. Modern Idiomatic Java Code Implementation

```java
// Comprehensive demonstration of buffered streams, serialization, and transient fields
package devtype.engine.io;

import java.io.*;

public final class StreamSerializationLab {

    public static class UserSession implements Serializable {
        @Serial
        private static final long serialVersionUID = 1L;

        private final String username;
        private transient String temporarySessionToken; // Excluded from serialization

        public UserSession(String username, String token) {
            this.username = username;
            this.temporarySessionToken = token;
        }

        public String getUsername() { return username; }
        public String getToken() { return temporarySessionToken; }
    }

    public static byte[] serializeSession(UserSession session) throws IOException {
        ByteArrayOutputStream byteOut = new ByteArrayOutputStream(512);
        try (ObjectOutputStream objOut = new ObjectOutputStream(new BufferedOutputStream(byteOut))) {
            objOut.writeObject(session);
        }
        return byteOut.toByteArray();
    }
}
```

---

## 5. Common Traps, Anti-Patterns & Best Practices

1. **Unbuffered Stream Syscall Inefficiency:**
   - *Trap:* Reading bytes directly via `FileInputStream.read()` issues an OS kernel system call for *every single byte*, causing massive performance degradation.
   - *Remedy:* Always wrap raw streams in `BufferedInputStream` or `BufferedReader`.

2. **Omitting `serialVersionUID`:**
   - *Trap:* If omitted, the compiler synthesizes one based on class structure. Any benign modification to fields invalidates existing serialized data with `InvalidClassException`.
   - *Remedy:* Always declare `private static final long serialVersionUID = 1L;`.

code:
package devtype.engine.io;

import java.io.*;

public final class StreamSerializationLab {

    public static class UserSession implements Serializable {
        @Serial
        private static final long serialVersionUID = 1L;

        private final String username;
        private transient String temporarySessionToken;

        public UserSession(String username, String token) {
            this.username = username;
            this.temporarySessionToken = token;
        }

        public String getUsername() { return username; }
        public String getToken() { return temporarySessionToken; }
    }

    public static byte[] serializeSession(UserSession session) throws IOException {
        ByteArrayOutputStream byteOut = new ByteArrayOutputStream(512);
        try (ObjectOutputStream objOut = new ObjectOutputStream(new BufferedOutputStream(byteOut))) {
            objOut.writeObject(session);
        }
        return byteOut.toByteArray();
    }
}

