# Module 15: NIO.2 File System — Paths, Files API CRUD & Memory-Efficient Streaming

> **Source Track:** Oracle Java Tutorials — *Essential Java Classes*  
> **Source Lessons:** `io/fileio.html`, `path.html`, `pathClass.html`, `fileOps.html`, `check.html`, `delete.html`, `copy.html`, `move.html`, `fileAttr.html`, `file.html`  
> **Scope:** `java.nio.file.Path`, `java.nio.file.Files`, Atomic Operations, File Attributes (`BasicFileAttributes`), Memory-Efficient Lazy Streaming (`Files.lines()`).

---

## 1. Architectural Overview: The NIO.2 Filesystem API

Introduced in Java 7 (`java.nio.file`), NIO.2 completely replaces the legacy `java.io.File` with an extensible, high-performance filesystem abstraction:
- **`Path`:** A platform-independent hierarchical locator representing a file or directory path.
- **`Files`:** A rich utility class consisting exclusively of static methods operating on `Path` instances.
- **Atomic File Operations:** Direct support for atomic moves and symbolic links.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Path["java.nio.file.Path (Platform-independent locator)"]
    Files["java.nio.file.Files Static Utilities"]
    Ops["File CRUD, Atomic Move, Attributes, Lazy Line Streaming"]

    Path --> Files --> Ops
```

---

## 2. Path Operations: Resolve, Relativize & Normalize

- **Resolve (Concatenation):** `basePath.resolve("sub/file.txt")` joins path segments.
- **Relativize:** `pathA.relativize(pathB)` calculates the relative navigation needed to reach `pathB` from `pathA`.
- **Normalize:** `path.normalize()` eliminates redundant `.` and `..` tokens.

---

## 3. Exhaustive Member Function & Files Reference

| Category | Method / Syntax (Example) | Use Case (Code Snippet) | Complexity | Description & Behavioral Invariant |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Path Builder** | `Path.of("a", "b")` | `Path p = Path.of("var", "logs", "app.log");` | $O(1)$ object build | Constructs platform-independent path. |
| **Check Exists** | `Files.exists(path)` | `if (Files.exists(p) && Files.isRegularFile(p))`| Native OS stat | Verifies existence without following symlinks if specified. |
| **Atomic Move** | `Files.move(s, d, opt)`| `Files.move(src, dst, StandardCopyOption.ATOMIC_MOVE);`| Native rename | Performs atomic filesystem rename. |
| **Create Directories**| `Files.createDirectories(p)`| `Files.createDirectories(targetDir);` | $O(D)$ mkdir -p | Recursively creates all non-existent parent directories. |
| **Lazy Lines Stream** | `Files.lines(path)` | `try (Stream<String> s = Files.lines(p)) { ... }`| $O(1)$ lazy memory | Streams lines lazily from disk without buffering entire file in heap. |
| **Write String** | `Files.writeString(p, s)`| `Files.writeString(p, data, StandardOpenOption.CREATE);`| $O(N)$ write | Convenient Java 11+ utility for writing text files via UTF-8. |

---

## 4. Modern Idiomatic Java Code Implementation

```java
// Comprehensive demonstration of NIO.2 Path operations, atomic moves, and lazy line streaming
package devtype.engine.io;

import java.io.IOException;
import java.nio.file.*;
import java.util.stream.Stream;

public final class ModernNioLab {

    public static long countErrorOccurrences(Path logPath, String keyword) throws IOException {
        // Memory-efficient lazy line streaming
        try (Stream<String> lines = Files.lines(logPath)) {
            return lines.filter(line -> line.contains(keyword)).count();
        }
    }

    public static void saveConfigSafely(Path targetFile, String data) throws IOException {
        Path tempFile = Files.createTempFile(targetFile.getParent(), "cfg-", ".tmp");
        Files.writeString(tempFile, data, StandardOpenOption.TRUNCATE_EXISTING);
        // Atomic filesystem rename replaces target cleanly
        Files.move(tempFile, targetFile, StandardCopyOption.ATOMIC_MOVE, StandardCopyOption.REPLACE_EXISTING);
    }
}
```

---

## 5. Common Traps, Anti-Patterns & Best Practices

1. **Leaking File Descriptors with `Files.lines()`:**
   - *Trap:* Invoking `Files.lines(path)` without wrapping it in a try-with-resources statement leaves the underlying file descriptor open.
   - *Remedy:* Always use `try (Stream<String> lines = Files.lines(path))`.

code:
