# Module 18: Concurrency II — Liveness Hazards, Deadlock & Guarded Blocks

> **Source Track:** Oracle Java Tutorials — *Essential Java Classes*  
> **Source Lessons:** `concurrency/liveness.html`, `deadlock.html`, `starvelive.html`, `guardmeth.html`, `immutable.html`  
> **Scope:** Liveness Hazards (Deadlock Coffman Conditions, Starvation, Livelock), Lock Ordering Protocols, Guarded Blocks (`wait()` inside `while` loop), Spurious Wakeup Prevention, `notifyAll()`.

---

## 1. Architectural Overview: Liveness Hazards

A concurrent application must maintain **Liveness**: ensuring that every thread makes forward execution progress.
1. **Deadlock:** Two or more threads are blocked forever, waiting for locks held by each other.
2. **Starvation:** A thread is perpetually denied CPU time or lock acquisition because greedy threads monopolize the resource.
3. **Livelock:** Threads actively change state in response to each other, but make zero forward progress.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    subgraph DeadlockCycle ["Deadlock Circular Dependency (Coffman Condition)"]
        T1["Thread 1 (Holds Lock A)"] -->|Requests Lock B| L2["Lock B"]
        T2["Thread 2 (Holds Lock B)"] -->|Requests Lock A| L1["Lock A"]
        L2 -.-> T2
        L1 -.-> T1
    end
```

---

## 2. Guarded Blocks: The `wait()` in `while` Invariant

Inter-thread coordination relies on **Guarded Blocks**:
```java
synchronized (lock) {
    while (!condition) {
        lock.wait(); // Releases lock and suspends thread
    }
    // Condition confirmed: proceed with critical section
}
```

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Never test a guarded condition with `if (condition)`!**  
> Always use a **`while` loop**. This protects against **Spurious Wakeups** (the OS waking the thread without a notify) and race conditions where another thread consumed the state before the woken thread could re-acquire the lock.

---

## 3. Exhaustive Member Function & Guarded Reference

| Category | Method / Syntax (Example) | Use Case (Code Snippet) | Complexity | Description & Behavioral Invariant |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Guarded Wait** | `lock.wait()` | `while (!ready) { lock.wait(); }` | O(1) deschedule | Releases monitor lock and suspends thread until notified. |
| **Notify All** | `lock.notifyAll()` | `lock.notifyAll();` | O(K) wakeups | Wakes all threads currently waiting on the object monitor. |
| **Notify Single**| `lock.notify()` | `lock.notify();` | O(1) wakeup | Wakes a single arbitrarily chosen thread waiting on monitor. |

---

## 4. Modern Idiomatic Java Code Implementation

```java
// Comprehensive demonstration of guarded blocks preventing spurious wakeups
package devtype.engine.concurrency;

public final class GuardedBlockLab {

    public static class BoundedBuffer<T> {
        private T data;
        private boolean empty = true;

        public synchronized void put(T item) throws InterruptedException {
            while (!empty) {
                wait(); // Wait until consumer empties buffer
            }
            this.data = item;
            this.empty = false;
            notifyAll(); // Signal consumer
        }

        public synchronized T take() throws InterruptedException {
            while (empty) {
                wait(); // Wait until producer populates buffer
            }
            T item = this.data;
            this.empty = true;
            notifyAll(); // Signal producer
            return item;
        }
    }
}
```

---

## 5. Common Traps, Anti-Patterns & Best Practices

1. **Calling `wait()` or `notify()` Outside a Synchronized Context:**
   - *Trap:* Invoking `obj.wait()` without holding `synchronized(obj)` throws `IllegalMonitorStateException`.
   - *Remedy:* Always synchronize on the target monitor before calling wait/notify.

code:
package devtype.engine.concurrency;

public final class GuardedBlockLab {

    public static class BoundedBuffer<T> {
        private T data;
        private boolean empty = true;

        public synchronized void put(T item) throws InterruptedException {
            while (!empty) {
                wait(); // Wait until buffer emptied (guards against spurious wakeups)
            }
            this.data = item;
            this.empty = false;
            notifyAll();
        }

        public synchronized T take() throws InterruptedException {
            while (empty) {
                wait(); // Wait until buffer populated
            }
            T item = this.data;
            this.empty = true;
            notifyAll();
            return item;
        }
    }
}

