# Module 19: Concurrency III — ReentrantLock, Executor Pools, ForkJoin & Atomic CAS

> **Source Track:** Oracle Java Tutorials — *Essential Java Classes*  
> **Source Lessons:** `concurrency/highlevel.html`, `newlocks.html`, `executors.html`, `pools.html`, `forkjoin.html`, `collections.html`, `atomicvars.html`, `threadlocalrandom.html`  
> **Scope:** Explicit Locks (`ReentrantLock`), Timed `tryLock()`, `ExecutorService` Thread Pools, Graceful Shutdown, `ForkJoinPool` Work-Stealing, Atomic CAS Variables (`AtomicInteger`), `ConcurrentHashMap`.

---

## 1. Architectural Overview: The `java.util.concurrent` Ecosystem

Modern Java concurrency avoids low-level `Thread` creation and `synchronized` blocks in favor of high-performance utilities:
1. **Explicit Locks (`ReentrantLock`):** Provide timed lock polling (`tryLock`), interruptible locks, and fairness policies.
2. **Executors & Thread Pools:** Decouple task submission from thread management.
3. **Lock-Free Atomic Variables:** Hardware-level Compare-And-Swap (CAS) instructions eliminating lock contention.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    JUC["java.util.concurrent Framework"]
    JUC --> Locks["ReentrantLock & ReadWriteLock"]
    JUC --> Exec["ExecutorService & ForkJoinPool"]
    JUC --> CAS["AtomicInteger / AtomicReference (Lock-Free)"]
    JUC --> Coll["ConcurrentHashMap (Lock-Striped)"]
```

---

## 2. Exhaustive Member Function & JUC Reference

| Category | Method / Syntax (Example) | Use Case (Code Snippet) | Complexity | Description & Behavioral Invariant |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Timed TryLock** | `lock.tryLock(t, u)` | `if (lock.tryLock(2, TimeUnit.SECONDS))` | Timed acquisition | Attempts lock acquisition within deadline, avoiding deadlocks. |
| **Executor Submit** | `exec.submit(callable)`| `Future<V> f = pool.submit(task);` | O(1) queue push | Submits task and returns a `Future` handle. |
| **Atomic CAS** | `atomic.incrementAndGet()`| `int next = seq.incrementAndGet();` | O(1) lock-free | Hardware atomic increment using Compare-And-Swap. |
| **Concurrent Map** | `map.putIfAbsent(k, v)`| `map.computeIfAbsent(k, f);` | O(1) bucket lock | Thread-safe associative map without global locking. |

---

## 3. Modern Idiomatic Java Code Implementation

```java
// Comprehensive demonstration of ExecutorService, ReentrantLock, and Atomic CAS
package devtype.engine.concurrency;

import java.util.concurrent.*;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.AtomicLong;
import java.util.concurrent.locks.ReentrantLock;

public final class AdvancedConcurrencyLab {

    public static class MetricsCollector {
        private final AtomicLong counter = new AtomicLong(0);
        public long recordRequest() { return counter.incrementAndGet(); } // Hardware CAS
        public long getCount() { return counter.get(); }
    }

    public static void runExecutorTasks() {
        ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(4);
        try {
            for (int i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
                final int id = i;
                pool.submit(() -> System.out.println("Running task: " + id));
            }
        } finally {
            pool.shutdown();
        }
    }
}
```

---

## 4. Common Traps, Anti-Patterns & Best Practices

1. **Calling `Future.get()` in Submit Loops:**
   - *Trap:* Invoking `future.get()` immediately after submitting a task blocks the caller, serializing what was intended to be concurrent execution.
   - *Remedy:* Submit all tasks first, collect the list of `Future` handles, then await completion.

