# Module 23: Regular Expressions II — Capturing Groups, Boundary Matchers & Matcher Pipelines

> **Source Track:** Oracle Java Tutorials — *Essential Java Classes*  
> **Source Lessons:** `regex/groups.html`, `bounds.html`, `pattern.html`, `matcher.html`, `unicode.html`  
> **Scope:** Numbered & Named Capturing Groups, Non-Capturing Groups, Boundary Matchers (`\b`, `^`, `$`), `Matcher` Search & Streaming Replace (`appendReplacement`, `appendTail`).

---

## 1. Architectural Overview: Capturing Groups & Boundaries

- **Numbered Groups:** Enclosed in `(...)`. Group 0 is the full match; group 1 is the first group.
- **Named Groups (Java 7+):** Enclosed in `(?<name>...)`; extracted via `matcher.group("name")`.
- **Non-Capturing Groups:** `(?:...)` groups elements without allocation overhead.
- **Boundary Matchers:** `\b` (word boundary), `^` (line start), `$` (line end), `\A` (input start), `\z` (input end).

---

## 2. Exhaustive Member Function & Matcher Reference

| Category | Method / Syntax (Example) | Use Case (Code Snippet) | Complexity | Description & Behavioral Invariant |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Matcher Find** | `matcher.find()` | `while (matcher.find()) { ... }` | O(N) scan | Finds next matching substring in input sequence. |
| **Matcher Matches** | `matcher.matches()` | `if (matcher.matches()) { ... }` | O(N) full match | Verifies whether the ENTIRE string matches the pattern. |
| **Named Group** | `matcher.group("id")` | `String id = matcher.group("id");` | O(1) read | Extracts substring captured by the named group. |
| **Append Replacement**| `m.appendReplacement(sb, r)`| `m.appendReplacement(sb, "MASKED");` | O(Segment) write | Streams non-matched prefix + replacement into buffer. |
| **Append Tail** | `m.appendTail(sb)` | `m.appendTail(sb);` | O(Trailing) write | Appends remaining un-scanned text following last match. |

---

## 3. Modern Idiomatic Java Code Implementation

```java
// Comprehensive demonstration of named capturing groups and streaming replace
package devtype.engine.regex;

import java.util.regex.*;

public final class RegexPipelinesLab {

    private static final Pattern EMAIL_PATTERN = Pattern.compile(
            "(?<user>[a-zA-Z0-9._%+-]+)@(?<domain>[a-zA-Z0-9.-]+\\.[a-zA-Z]{2,})"
    );

    public static String maskEmails(String text) {
        Matcher matcher = EMAIL_PATTERN.matcher(text);
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(text.length() + 32);

        while (matcher.find()) {
            String user = matcher.group("user");
            String domain = matcher.group("domain");
            String masked = user.charAt(0) + "***@" + domain;
            matcher.appendReplacement(sb, Matcher.quoteReplacement(masked));
        }
        matcher.appendTail(sb);
        return sb.toString();
    }
}
```

---

## 4. Common Traps, Anti-Patterns & Best Practices

1. **Forgetting `matcher.appendTail(sb)`:**
   - *Trap:* Omitting `appendTail` discards all text in the source string after the final match.
   - *Remedy:* Always follow `appendReplacement` loops with `matcher.appendTail(sb)`.

